kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.record-257bm-k2"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.record-257bm-k2"],"id":"long.record-257bm-k2","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Proposition 6.129 (A sufficient fractional-mass bound need not hold)","locator":"Long record Proposition 6.129, p. 80, label record:257bm-k2","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Take $D=\\{2,3\\}$ and $c=5$. Direct calculation gives\n$$\nX_D(2)=\\frac{10}{21}<\\frac12\n <\\frac{331}{651}=X_{D\\cup\\{5\\}}(2),\\qquad S(D,1,8)=6<8.\n$$\nHowever, the combined fractional mass is\n$$\n\\sum_{d\\in D\\cup\\{5\\}}\\frac{2^8\\bmod(2^d-1)}{2^d-1}\n =\\frac{757}{651}>1.\n$$\nThe linked Lean proof checks this example by direct computation. Thus the combined fractional-mass bound by $1$, which suffices for the sharp capacity estimate, is not necessary for that estimate. Thus the fractional-mass bound cannot hold at every real crossing core. A proof using it would need to handle the exceptional cores separately; the example does not rule out all uses of fractional-mass estimates. Theorem 6.133 identifies the support at a critical crossing with a real greedy prefix, but does not prove the missing bound on those prefixes.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.record-257bm-k2","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.record-257bm-k2","id":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.record-257bm-k2%22%2C%22lean.ErdosProblems.Erdos257.PaperCompleteR21.paper_fractional_mass_bound_not_necessary%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.record-257bm-k2%22%2C%22lean.ErdosProblems.Erdos257.PaperCompleteR21.paper_fractional_mass_bound_not_necessary%22%2C%22formaliz